  "query_preview": "..(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 430 AM EDT SAT AUG 29 2026\n\nThe most current model data continues to suggest that the current \nforecast is on track for a dry and gradually warming period into\nMid week with low to mid 90s on tap for highs to start as we are a\nbit warmer each day. We still see the potential for convection in\na few of the model solutions across forecast area on Thursday and\nFriday - mainly in the afternoon and evening hours with better\nsupport from the ECMWF runs than the GFS at this point. Heat \nheadlines may be needed for at least western parts of the area at \nmidweek - depending on how effectively the higher dewpoints can \nmove east into this part of the state. \n\nThe previous long term discussion follows:\n\nSummer saw September coming and said hold my drink, I've got some \nfight left in me. The story of the extended period is the Death \nRidge that sets up across Kentucky and points south starting on \nSunday and continuing until at least mid-week next week. In addition \nto this increasing level of subsidence, surface winds will also be \nout of the SW across Kentucky due to a surface high pressure just to \nour east, allowing return flow and WAA into the region. Both \ncombined will mean sunny, hot, and humid conditions. Upper 80s over \nthe weekend will make way for low to mid 90s through much of the \nwork week. Humidity will only insult things further, as heat indices \nare forecast to register in the upper 90s, even topping 100 degrees \nin some locations. Overnight lows will also gradually trend warmer \nthroughout the work week, starting in the upper 60s over the weekend \nand ending in the low to mid 70s. Even if the forecast modifies \nslightly as we get closer in time, the numbers changing a couple \ndegrees won't change the fact that it is going to be hot next week. \n\nAs far as precipitation, the strong subsidence should keep us dry \nthrough mid-week. As we head into Wednesday, we do have some slight \nchances of precipitation returning according to the NBM. However, \nlooking at the models, the ridge is still very much on top of us, \nand none of the models are really showing any rain for the state. \nTherefore, expect that the NBM, despite it being low chances, may \nstill be overdone. By Thursday, the models start to diverge in their \nsolutions. The ECMWF shows the ridge finally breaking down across \nKentucky, with NW flow returning and a surface system getting \nsteered SE straight into the Ohio Valley with likely precipitation. \nMeanwhile, the GFS still shows big bubble no trouble overhead of \nKentucky, with no precipitation in sight. Trying to blend between\nthese two opposite model solutions, the NBM has gone with slight \nchances in some locations, ramping up to chances in the afternoon \nin the far east. Just like the temperatures, expect these pops to \nchange and modify some as we get closer to onset and the models \nhopefully come into better agreement.",
